L000169	\title{
L000170	Does hot QCD have a conformal manifold in the chiral limit?
L000171	}
L000172	
L000173	\author{Shi Chen \orcidlink{0000-0002-8554-5098}}
L000174	\email{s.chern.phys@gmail.com}
L000175	\affiliation{School of Physics and Astronomy, University of Minnesota, Minneapolis, MN 55455, USA}
L000176	\author{Aleksey Cherman \orcidlink{0000-0002-1039-8476}}
L000177	\email{acherman@umn.edu}
L000178	\affiliation{School of Physics and Astronomy, University of Minnesota, Minneapolis, MN 55455, USA}
L000179	\author{Robert D. Pisarski \orcidlink{0000-0002-7862-4759}}
L000180	\email{pisarski@bnl.gov}
L000181	\affiliation{Physics Department, Brookhaven National Laboratory,
L000182	Upton, NY 11973-5000}
L000183	
L000184	\date{\today}
L000185	
L000186	\begin{abstract}
L000187	Recent lattice evidence suggests the chiral phase transition in QCD is second-order for $N_f \ge 2$ massless flavors. We
L000188	constrain CFT descriptions of a critical line in
L000189	temperature $T$ and imaginary baryon chemical potential $\theta_B =
L000190	i\mu_B/T$. An 't~Hooft anomaly at general $\theta_B$ constrains the
L000191	transition even at $\theta_B = 0$, leaving only three minimal scenarios. The
L000192	best-motivated scenario for $N_f\ge3$, and perhaps also $N_f = 2$, is beyond
L000193	Ginzburg-Landau, featuring a conformal manifold of
L000194	$\theta_B$-dependent universality classes with an exactly marginal
L000195	operator related to baryon density.
L000196	\end{abstract}
L000197	
L000198	\maketitle
L000199	
L000200	
L000201	
L000202	{\bf Introduction.}
L000203	
L000204	
L000205	Understanding the phase diagram of quantum chromodynamics (QCD) as a
L000206	function of temperature $T$ and baryon chemical potential $\mu_B$ is a
L000207	central challenge in high-energy physics,
L000208	with implications for heavy-ion collisions, astrophysics, and the early
L000209	universe.
L000210	At $\mu_B=0$, increasing $T$ restores the spontaneously broken approximate
L000211	chiral symmetry through a crossover at $T \sim 155\,
L000212	\textrm{MeV}$~\cite{HotQCD:2018pds,Borsanyi:2020fev}.
L000213	If $N_f$ quarks are massless, chiral symmetry becomes exact, and the crossover
L000214	becomes a genuine phase transition. The standard approach to this
L000215	nonperturbative phase transition appeals to Landau universality. A
L000216	Ginzburg-Landau analysis based on the chiral condensate $\Phi = \bar{\psi}_L
L000217	\psi_R$~\cite{Pisarski:1983ms} suggests a first-order transition for $N_f\geq3$,
L000218	and a second-order transition for $N_f\!=\!2$,
L000219	in the $O(4)$ Wilson-Fisher universality class.
L000220	
L000221	Early lattice simulations seemed to agree with these
L000222	predictions~\cite{Brown:1990ev,Karsch:1994hm,Iwasaki:1995ij,JLQCD:1998qth,
L000223	Karsch:2001nf,Liao:2001en,deForcrand:2003vyj,Jin:2014hea}, but recent lattice
L000224	simulations~\cite{Cuteri:2021ikv,Dini:2021hug,Nakamura:2022abk,Zhang:2022kzb,Zhang:2024ldl,Zhang:2025vns,Klinger:2025xxb,DAmbrosio:2025ldv,Klinger:2026pbe} attribute
L000225	the earlier $N_f \ge 3$ results to discretization artifacts and find no
L000226	evidence of a first-order transition for any $N_f \ge 2$ at $\mu_B=0$ or
L000227	imaginary $\mu_B$~\cite{DAmbrosio:2025ldv}. Lattice results thus suggest
L000228	that the chiral transition is second-order, or at most weakly
L000229	first-order, from $N_f = 2$ up to the onset of the conformal window\cite{Klinger:2026pbe}, at $\mu_B = 0$ and along a line
L000230	extending to imaginary $\mu_B$.
L000231	
L000232	Various theoretical interpretations of the recent lattice results have been proposed~\cite{Fejos:2022mso,Bernhardt:2023hpr,Fejos:2024bgl,Giacosa:2024orp,Pisarski:2024esv}, but compelling solutions remain elusive.
L000233	Here we systematically constrain the 3D conformal field theories (CFTs) that can appear on a
L000234	natural critical line of chiral transitions in the $\theta_B$-$T$ phase
L000235	diagram, with real $\theta_B= i\mu_B / T$. We find only three minimal
L000236	scenarios compatible with the ’t Hooft
L000237	anomaly~\cite{Yonekura:2019vyz,Nishimura:2019umw,Kobayashi:2023ajk},
L000238	high-$T$ perturbative calculations~\cite{Roberge:1986mm}, and low-$T$
L000239	QCD phenomenology, shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:allowed_scenarios}.
L000240	
L000241	\begin{figure*}[ht]
L000242	  \centering
L000243	  \includegraphics[width=0.3\textwidth]{figures/all_broken_standalone.pdf}
L000244	  \hfill
L000245	  \includegraphics[width=0.3\textwidth]{figures/dqcp_standalone.pdf}
L000246	  \hfill
L000247	  \includegraphics[width=0.3\textwidth]{figures/conformal_manifold_standalone.pdf}
L000248	  \caption{Minimal phase diagrams of massless QCD consistent with a natural second-order
L000249	   line of chiral phase transitions. \textbf{Left}: Landau scenario.
L000250	   \textbf{Middle}: Landau-DQCP scenario. \textbf{Right}: Conformal-manifold
L000251	   scenario. The conformal manifold scenario appears plausible for all
L000252	   $N_f \ge 2$, while the Landau and Landau-DQCP scenarios are only
L000253	   plausible for $N_f=2$.}
L000254	  \label{fig:allowed_scenarios}
L000255	\end{figure*}
L000256	
L000257	In both the Landau (left) and Landau-DQCP (middle) scenarios, the critical
L000258	line flows to a single Ginzburg-Landau CFT for generic $\theta_B$, but the
L000259	latter also features a deconfined quantum critical point
L000260	(DQCP)~\cite{Senthil:2003eed,Senthil:2004fuw,Senthil:2023vqd} at $\theta_B =
L000261	\pi$. These scenarios are conceivable for $N_f=2$, but for $N_f \ge 3$ they
L000262	require a natural fixed point in the Ginzburg-Landau description of
L000263	Ref.~\cite{Pisarski:1983ms} that has not been found despite extensive
L000264	searches. In the conformal-manifold scenario (right), the critical line
L000265	is a continuum of distinct beyond-Landau 3D CFTs. We argue
L000266	that this scenario is supported by a $(2+\epsilon)$D expansion and existing
L000267	conformal bootstrap bounds for $N_f \ge 2$. So the answer to the title's
L000268	question could be \emph{yes}.
L000269	
L000270	
L000271	
L000272	{\bf Symmetry and anomaly.}
L000273	
L000274	
L000275	We work in Euclidean signature throughout.
L000276	Massless QCD, a 4D $SU(N_c)$ gauge theory for $N_c\ge3$ with $N_f$ massless fundamental Dirac fermions, has the continuous global symmetry
L000277	\begin{align}
L000278	\begin{split}
L000279	    G = &\:\frac{U(1)_B \times SU(N_f)_L \times SU(N_f)_R }{\Z_{N_f}},
L000280	\end{split}
L000281	\label{eq:QCD_symmetry}
L000282	\end{align}
L000283	where $\Z_{N_f}$ sets $(\e^{2\pi i N_c/N_f}, \e^{-2\pi i/N_f}
L000284	\mathbf{1}_{N_f}, \e^{-2\pi i/N_f} \mathbf{1}_{N_f})$ to  the identity.
L000285	Here $U(1)_B=U(1)_V/\Z_{N_c}$ is the baryon symmetry, while
L000286	$SU(N_f)_{L} \times SU(N_f)_R$ is the chiral symmetry.
L000287	The $U(1)_A$ symmetry of the classical theory is destroyed by the axial anomaly, and so it is not part of $G$.
L000288	The discrete global symmetry acts non-trivially on $G$ and is generated by charge conjugation $\calC$ and geometric reflections, including time reversal.
L000289	
L000290	The symmetry $G$ has an 't Hooft anomaly~\cite{tHooft:1979rat}. Turning
L000291	on background gauge fields $A_B$, $A_L$, $A_R$ for $U(1)_B$,
L000292	$SU(N_f)_L$, $SU(N_f)_R$, respectively, leads to an anomalous
L000293	background gauge variation described by a 5D invertible field
L000294	theory~\cite{Freed:2014iua,Kapustin:2014lwa,Witten:2015aba,Yonekura:2016wuc,Bhardwaj:2023kri}
L000295	with partition function
L000296	\begin{align}\label{eq:SPT_4d}
L000297	\begin{gathered}
L000298	    \mathcal{I}_{5D}(A_B,A_L,A_R) = \\
L000299	    \mathcal{J}_{5D}^{N_c}(A_L)\mathcal{J}_{5D}^{-N_c}(A_R)
L000300	    \exp\!\left[\frac{i}{8\pi^2} \!\int_{5D}\!\! A_B \tr( F_L^2 - F_R^2)\right]\!,
L000301	\end{gathered}
L000302	\end{align}
L000303	where $F_{L,R}$ is $A_{L,R}$'s field strength.
L000304	For $N_f \ge 3$, $\calJ_{5D}(A)$ is the 5D Chern-Simons partition
L000305	function~\cite{Wess:1971yu}, while for $N_f=2$ it is the 5D spin
L000306	topological invariant associated with the Witten
L000307	anomaly~\cite{Witten:1982fp,Wang:2018qoy}.
L000308	
L000309	As a renormalization group (RG) invariant, the 't Hooft anomaly~\eqref{eq:SPT_4d} ensures massless excitations~\cite{Frishman:1980dq,Coleman:1982yg}, but their nature and interactions depend on $N_c$ and $N_f$.
L000310	Below the conformal window (very roughly
L000311	$N_f\lesssim\frac{8}{3}N_c$~\cite{LatticeStrongDynamics:2018hun,Kuti:2022ldb,Ingoldby:2023mtf,Hasenfratz:2023wbr,Hasenfratz:2024fad,Bergner:2025yke}),
L000312	the continuous symmetry $G$ is spontaneously broken to
L000313	\begin{equation}
L000314	    H = \frac{U(1)_B\times SU(N_f)_V}{\Z_{N_f}}\,,
L000315	\end{equation}
L000316	while the discrete symmetries remain intact.
L000317	The gapless IR physics is described via a nonlinear scalar field
L000318	\begin{equation}\label{eq:U(x)}
L000319	    \phi(x) \ \in\ G/H\ \simeq\ SU(N_f)\,.
L000320	\end{equation}
L000321	In this phase, $\mathcal{J}^{N_c}(A_L)\mathcal{J}^{-N_c}(A_R)$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:SPT_4d} is matched by the Wess-Zumino-Witten action~\cite{Witten:1983tw} for $N_f\ge3$, and by the discrete spin $\theta$-angle~\cite{Witten:1983tw} for $N_f=2$.
L000322	The $A_B$ term is matched by identifying the Skyrme current (from $\pi_3(SU(N_f)) = \Z$) with the baryon current~\cite{Witten:1983tx}.
L000323	
L000324	
L000325	
L000326	{\bf Circle compactification.}
L000327	
L000328	
L000329	We now put massless QCD on $\R^3\times S^1$ with circumference $\beta$.
L000330	An $S^1$-homogeneous gauge field splits into a gauge field on $\R^3$
L000331	and a holonomy scalar field on $\R^3$. In particular, the $U(1)_B$
L000332	holonomy gives a $2\pi$-periodic scalar field $\theta_B$.
L000333	
L000334	\footnote{What directly shows up in the QCD Lagrangian is $U(1)_V$ instead of $U(1)_B$. Thus in
L000335	the path integral, we have to define $\theta_B$ via $\theta_B =\theta_VN_c$.
L000336	Then the shift
L000337	$\theta_B\sim\theta_B+2\pi$ is generated by an improper $SU(N_c)$ gauge transformation $g$ that
L000338	satisfies $g(x,\tau+\beta) = g(x,\tau)\,\e^{2\pi i/N_c}$ for $\tau\in
L000339	S^1$~\cite{Roberge:1986mm}.}
L000340	
L000341	For constant $\theta_B$, the circle compactification has two Hamiltonian
L000342	interpretations, and all physical observables, including the free energy
L000343	density
L000344	\begin{equation}
L000345	    \label{eq:free_energy}
L000346	    f_{\beta}(\theta_B)\equiv-\lim_{V\to\infty}\frac{1}{\beta V}\ln Z_{\beta}(\theta_B),
L000347	\end{equation}
L000348	must be \emph{simultaneously} interpretable in both pictures:
L000349	\begin{itemize}
L000350	    \item \textbf{(Thermal)} Hamiltonian evolution along $S^1$ yields a grand
L000351	    canonical ensemble on $\R^3$ with temperature $T=1/\beta$ and baryonic chemical potential $\mu_B =
L000352	    -iT\theta_B$. Then $-f_{\beta}(\theta_B)$ is the thermal pressure.
L000353	    \item \textbf{(Quantum)} Hamiltonian evolution along $\R$ yields a
L000354	    zero-temperature quantum system on $\R^2\!\times\!S^1$ with
L000355	    circumference $\beta$ and a baryonic Aharonov-Bohm phase
L000356	    $e^{i\theta_B}$. Then $f_{\beta}(\theta_B)$ is the Casimir energy
L000357	    density.
L000358	\end{itemize}
L000359	Therefore, with real $\theta_B\neq0$, we lose reflection positivity
L000360	reflecting $S^1$, but preserve reflection positivity reflecting an
L000361	$\R \subset \R^3$.
L000362	The imaginary baryon density at zero Matsubara frequency,
L000363	\begin{equation}\label{eq:baryon_density}
L000364	    \rho_B(x) \equiv \frac{i}{\beta}\int_{0}^{\beta}\!\!\!\d\tau\, j_B^0(x,\tau)\,,
L000365	\end{equation}
L000366	is anti-hermitian in the thermal picture, but is hermitian in the quantum picture.
L000367	
L000368	The quantum picture is convenient for describing 3D infrared (IR)
L000369	effective theories at length scales $\ell \gg\beta$. These theories are
L000370	unitary 3D quantum field theories (QFTs) for any real $\theta_B$, and $\rho_B$ flows to a hermitian operator thereof. When the 3D effective
L000371	theory becomes scale-invariant at a critical point,
L000372	unitarity is expected to enhance scale invariance to full conformal invariance; see
L000373	e.g.~Refs.~\cite{Polchinski:1987dy,El-Showk:2011xbs,Nakayama:2013is,Delamotte:2015aaa,Paulos:2015jfa,Meneses:2018xpu}.
L000374	
L000375	Upon $S^1$ compactification, the 5D invertible theory~\eqref{eq:SPT_4d}
L000376	reduces to the 4D invertible theory
L000377	\begin{align}\label{eq:SPT_3D}
L000378	    \mathcal{I}_{4D}(\theta_B,A_L,A_R) =
L000379	    \exp\!\left[\frac{i}{8\pi^2}\! \int_{4D}\!\! \theta_B \,\tr (F_L^2 -F_R^2)\right]\!.
L000380	\end{align}
L000381	It is well-defined on closed 4D oriented manifolds, but is not
L000382	invariant under $\theta_B\to\theta_B+2\pi$ if the 4D manifold has a 3D
L000383	boundary. Hence, with $S^1$-homogeneous $A_{L,R}$, the partition
L000384	function of $S^1$-compactified massless QCD
L000385	satisfies~\cite{Yonekura:2019vyz,Nishimura:2019umw,Kobayashi:2023ajk}
L000386	\begin{align}\label{eq:anomaly_3D}
L000387	\begin{gathered}
L000388	    Z_{\beta}(\theta_B + 2\pi, A_L, A_R) \\
L000389	    = Z_{\beta}(\theta_B, A_L, A_R) \,\calJ_{3D}(A_L) \,\calJ_{3D}^{-1}(A_R)
L000390	\end{gathered}
L000391	\end{align}
L000392	where $\calJ_{3D}(A)$ is the 3D Chern-Simons partition function
L000393	\begin{align}
L000394	    \calJ_{3D}(A) =  \exp\left[\frac{\i}{4\pi}\int_{3D}
L000395	    \mathrm{tr}\left( A \d A - \i\frac{2}{3} A^3 \right)\right] \,.
L000396	\end{align}
L000397	This is a mixed anomaly between $SU(N_f)_{L,R}$ and a $2\pi$-periodic coupling~\cite{Kikuchi:2017pcp,Cordova:2019jnf,Cordova:2019uob,Heidenreich:2021xpr,Garcia-Valdecasas:2024cqn,Yu:2024jtk}, or a ``$(-1)$-form $U(1)$ symmetry''~\cite{Najjar:2024vmm,Aloni:2024jpb, Lin:2025oml,Robbins:2025urk}.
L000398	
L000399	Upon $S^1$ compactification, the discrete symmetry is generated by $\calC$, the $S^1$-reflection $\calS$, and $\R$-reflections $\calR$ for $\R\subset\R^3$.
L000400	They act on $S^1$-homogeneous 3D background fields as
L000401	\begin{equation}
L000402	\!\!\!\!\begin{gathered}
L000403	    \theta_B(x) \xrightarrow{\calC} -\theta_B(x),\quad A_{L,R}(x) \xrightarrow{\calC} -A_{R,L}^*(x),\\
L000404	    \theta_B(x) \xrightarrow{\calS} -\theta_B(x),\quad A_{L,R}(x) \xrightarrow{\calS} A_{R,L}(x),\\
L000405	    \theta_B(x) \xrightarrow{\calR}\theta_B(\overline{x}),\quad A_{L,R}(x) \xrightarrow{\calR} \overline{A}_{R,L}(\overline{x}),
L000406	\end{gathered}
L000407	\end{equation}
L000408	where the bars indicate an $\R$-reflection.
L000409	$\calC$, $\calS$, and $\calR$ exchange $L$ and $R$.
L000410	When $N_f=2$, $\calC\calS$ can be undone by $SU(N_f)_{L,R}$.
L000411	
L000412	A constant $\theta_B\neq0,\pi$ preserves only the subgroup generated by
L000413	$\calC\calS$ and $\calR$, whereas $\calS$, $\calC$, $\calS\calR$, and
L000414	$\calC\calR$ are restored at $\theta_B=\pi$ by the $2\pi$-periodicity
L000415	of $\theta_B$. Therefore, at $\theta_B=\pi$,
L000416	Eq.~\eqref{eq:anomaly_3D} gives a mixed anomaly between $SU(N_f)_{L,R}$
L000417	and $(\Z_2)_{\calS\calR}$~\cite{Yonekura:2019vyz,Nishimura:2019umw,Kobayashi:2023ajk}.
L000418	Equivalently, this is an anomaly of
L000419	$SU(N_f)_{L,R}\rtimes(\Z_2)_{\calC\calR}$, or of
L000420	$[SU(N_f)_L\times SU(N_f)_R]\rtimes(\Z_2)_{\calS}$ or
L000421	$\rtimes(\Z_2)_{\calC}$. The 4D invertible theory~\eqref{eq:SPT_3D} captures
L000422	this because, at $\theta_B=\pi$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:SPT_3D} can be defined on
L000423	unorientable manifolds or with twists that exchange $L$ and $R$.
L000424	
L000425	
L000426	
L000427	{\bf Low-$T$ and high-$T$ Phases.}
L000428	
L000429	
L000430	Although it does not guarantee gapless modes, the 3D
L000431	anomaly~\eqref{eq:SPT_3D} excludes $\theta_B$-uniform trivially gapped
L000432	phases, as well as a trivially gapped phase at $\theta_B=\pi$.
L000433	The 3D IR physics at length scales $\ell\gg\beta$ must see $\theta_B$ and the symmetry
L000434	\begin{equation}\label{eq:3D_IR_symmetry}
L000435	    \frac{SU(N_f)_L\times SU(N_f)_R}{\Z_{N_f}}
L000436	    \begin{cases}
L000437	        \!\rtimes (\Z_2)^3_{\calC,\calS,\calR}, & \theta_B=0,\pi \\
L000438	        \!\rtimes (\Z_2)^2_{\calC\calS,\calR}, & \theta_B\neq0,\pi
L000439	    \end{cases}
L000440	\end{equation}
L000441	where discrete symmetry needs to quotient out $(\Z_2)_{\calC\calS}$ for $N_f=2$.
L000442	The operator $\rho_B$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:baryon_density} is neutral under
L000443	the second line of Eq.~\eqref{eq:3D_IR_symmetry} but charged under the
L000444	first.
L000445	In contrast, the 3D baryon symmetry $U(1)_B$ and chiral holonomies $\theta_{L,R}$ need not appear in the IR.
L000446	
L000447	At sufficiently low $T$, the symmetry $G$ is expected to remain broken to
L000448	$H$ just as at $T=0$, and the IR physics is described by the 3D
L000449	reduction of the nonlinear scalar field $\phi(x)$ of
L000450	Eq.~\eqref{eq:U(x)}.
L000451	The 3D symmetry~\eqref{eq:3D_IR_symmetry} acts on it by $\phi(x)\to L\phi(x)R^{-1}$ for $L,R\in SU(N_f)$ and
L000452	\begin{equation}
L000453	    \phi(x) \xrightarrow{\calC} \phi^T(x)\,,\
L000454	    \phi(x) \xrightarrow{\calS} \phi^{\dag}(x)\,,\
L000455	    \phi(x) \xrightarrow{\calR} \phi^{\dag}(\overline{x})\,.
L000456	\end{equation}
L000457	When $N_f=2$, $\calC\calS$ is absorbed into $SU(2)_{L,R}$.
L000458	The low-$T$ 3D IR effective QFT is given by~\cite{Xu:2011sj,Yonekura:2019vyz}
L000459	\begin{equation}\label{eq:lowT_EFT}
L000460	\begin{gathered}
L000461	    \!\!\!\int\!\!\calD \phi\exp\!\left\{\!-\frac{1}{g^2}\!\!\int\!\!\d^3\!x\,\tr \partial_{a}\phi\, \partial^{a}\!\phi^{\dagger}\! + i\!\!\int\!\!\theta_B\frac{\tr(\phi^{\dagger}\d \phi)^3}{24\pi^2} \!\right\}\!.
L000462	\end{gathered}
L000463	\end{equation}
L000464	To verify anomaly matching in Eq.~\eqref{eq:lowT_EFT}, one can turn on background
L000465	gauge fields $A_{L,R}$ and study their interplay with the Skyrme 3-form~\cite{Witten:1983tw,Xu:2011sj,Yonekura:2019vyz}.
L000466	In this low-$T$ gapless phase, varying $\theta_B$ does not induce
L000467	phase transitions since baryons are heavy.
L000468	
L000469	At sufficiently high $T$, perturbative evaluations of some quantities
L000470	are trustworthy; see e.g.~Refs.~\cite{Gross:1980br,KapustaGale201102}.
L000471	They imply that chiral symmetry is restored and there is a mass gap for
L000472	all $\theta_B$~\cite{Roberge:1986mm}. Roberge
L000473	and Weiss~\cite{Roberge:1986mm} pointed out a first-order phase transition at $\theta_B=\pi$.
L000474	This is associated with discrete symmetry breaking at $\theta_B=\pi$,
L000475	\begin{equation}
L000476	\label{eq:broken_Z2}
L000477	    (\Z_2)^3_{\calC,\calS,\calR} \ \ \to\ \ (\Z_2)^2_{\calC\calS,\calR},
L000478	\end{equation}
L000479	which needs to quotient out $(\Z_2)_{\calC\calS}$ for $N_f=2$.
L000480	The order operator is $\rho_B$ with
L000481	\begin{equation}
L000482	    \langle\rho_B\rangle = \frac{\partial f_{\beta}(\theta_B)}{\partial\theta_B}\,.
L000483	\end{equation}
L000484	In this high-$T$ gapped phase, continuous chiral symmetry flows to an emergent $2$-form $U(1)$ symmetry~\cite{Gaiotto:2014kfa} and the 3D anomaly~\eqref{eq:SPT_3D} is matched via symmetry transmutation~\cite{Seiberg:2025bqy}; we explain this high-$T$ IR effective theory in the Supplemental Material~\ref{sec:high_T_EFT}.
L000485	
L000486	In summary, chiral symmetry is broken in the low-$T$ phase and restored
L000487	in the high-$T$ phase, while the discrete symmetry at $\theta_B=\pi$ is
L000488	broken
L000489	
L000490	\footnote{
L000491	If a CFT matching the symmetry and anomaly existed, a second-order transition at $\theta_B=\pi$ without symmetry breaking could occur at lower $T$ in the high-$T$ phase~\cite{Xu:2011sj,Yonekura:2019vyz,Nishimura:2019umw,Kobayashi:2023ajk}; see the Supplemental Material~\ref{sec:anomalons} for comments.}
L000492	
L000493	in the high-$T$ phase and restored in the low-$T$ phase. This
L000494	complementarity of symmetry realizations is a fingerprint of mixed
L000495	anomalies.
L000496	
L000497	
L000498	
L000499	{\bf Consistent phase diagrams.}
L000500	
L000501	
L000502	The low-$T$ and high-$T$ phases are separated by a critical line
L000503	$\theta_B \mapsto T_c(\theta_B)$. Motivated by recent lattice
L000504	results~\cite{Cuteri:2021ikv,Dini:2021hug,Nakamura:2022abk,Zhang:2022kzb,Zhang:2024ldl,Zhang:2025vns,Klinger:2025xxb,DAmbrosio:2025ldv,Klinger:2026pbe},
L000505	we postulate that this line generically consists of natural second-order
L000506	points with emergent scale invariance.
L000507	The IR physics of such a natural critical point is a 3D unitary CFT whose neutral
L000508	sector under the symmetry~\eqref{eq:3D_IR_symmetry} has precisely one
L000509	relevant primary scalar operator $\calO_{E}$, to which the $T$
L000510	perturbation flows. The operator $\rho_B$ also flows to
L000511	$\calO_{E}$ at $\theta_B \neq 0,\pi$, or at $\theta_B = 0,\pi$ if
L000512	spontaneous symmetry breaking~\eqref{eq:broken_Z2} occurs.
L000513	
L000514	The 't Hooft anomaly~\eqref{eq:SPT_3D} prohibits the critical line from
L000515	flowing to a single CFT for all $\theta_B$. First, to produce
L000516	the behavior~\eqref{eq:anomaly_3D}, the IR physics must change as we
L000517	smoothly vary $\theta_B$ to $\theta_B+2\pi$. Second, it must differ at
L000518	$\theta_B=0$ and $\pi$, since these points have the same symmetry but
L000519	distinct anomalies. Therefore, the IR physics must change from
L000520	$\theta_B=0$ to $\theta_B=\pi$, either abruptly or gradually. Abrupt
L000521	changes correspond to phase transitions at special $\theta_B$, like in
L000522	the high-$T$ phase. Gradual changes, as in the low-$T$ phase,
L000523	correspond to smooth variations of the CFT data along the critical line.
L000524	
L000525	If the changes in the IR physics are abrupt, a neighborhood of
L000526	$\theta_B=0$ on the critical line only cares about the breaking
L000527	$G \to H$, and should fit the Landau paradigm, i.e.~be described by a
L000528	natural fixed point of the Ginzburg-Landau model of
L000529	Ref.~\cite{Pisarski:1983ms}. Minimally, it suffices to have one
L000530	phase transition at $\theta_B = \pi$ and a
L000531	Ginzburg-Landau description for the rest of the critical line.
L000532	If the transition at $\theta_B = \pi$ is first-order ({\bf Landau
L000533	scenario}, Fig.~\ref{fig:allowed_scenarios} left), discrete
L000534	symmetry breaking~\eqref{eq:broken_Z2} with $\langle\rho_B\rangle\neq0$ occurs at $\theta_B = \pi$. There must then be a
L000535	window at $\theta_B=\pi$ where chiral and discrete symmetries are
L000536	broken, with an Ising transition at its lower edge.
L000537	
L000538	\footnote{Such a window is disfavored by current lattice evidence~\cite{Bonati:2018fvg,Cuteri:2022vwk}.}
L000539	If instead the
L000540	transition at $\theta_B = \pi$ is second order ({\bf Landau-DQCP
L000541	scenario}, Fig.~\ref{fig:allowed_scenarios} middle), it must be a
L000542	beyond-Landau critical point analogous to condensed-matter
L000543	DQCPs~\cite{Senthil:2003eed,Senthil:2004fuw,Senthil:2023vqd}. The
L000544	operator $\rho_B$ flows to a relevant (or marginally relevant) operator
L000545	at this DQCP.
L000546	
L000547	If the changes in the IR physics are gradual, the CFT data instead vary smoothly with $\theta_B$ on the critical line ({\bf conformal-manifold scenario}, Fig.~\ref{fig:allowed_scenarios} right).
L000548	This scenario requires a conformal manifold --- a continuum of distinct
L000549	CFTs --- generated by an exactly marginal operator $\calO_{B}$. The
L000550	operator $\rho_B$ flows to $\calO_{B}$ at $\theta_B=0,\pi$, and to a
L000551	linear combination of $\calO_{B}$ and $\calO_{E}$ at
L000552	$\theta_B\neq0,\pi$. The presence of $\calO_{B}$ makes this scenario
L000553	intrinsically beyond Ginzburg-Landau.
L000554	
L000555	One can imagine more complicated scenarios with phase transitions
L000556	away from $\theta_B=\pi$, but they generically require unnatural
L000557	multicritical points. In any case, the three scenarios above are the
L000558	minimal ones under the assumption of a second-order critical line.
L000559	
L000560	
L000561	
L000562	{\bf Landau scenarios.}
L000563	
L000564	
L000565	The Landau and Landau-DQCP scenarios posit that the critical line at
L000566	$\theta_B \neq \pi$ is described
L000567	
L000568	\footnote{
L000569	For $\theta_B\neq0$, we should allow chiral-symmetric $(\Z_2)^2_{\calC\calS,\calR}$-neutral $(\Z_2)^3_{\calC,\calS,\calR}$-charged deformations, such as $i\lambda_4 \int[\tr (\Phi^\dagger d \Phi)^3 + \text{c.c.}]$.
L000570	However, operators in this sector are expected to be irrelevant.}
L000571	
L000572	by a natural fixed point of the 3D linear sigma model of
L000573	Ref.~\cite{Pisarski:1983ms}:
L000574	\begin{align}\label{eq:Landau-Ginzburg}
L000575	\begin{gathered}
L000576	    \int\!\calD\Phi\,\exp\Big\{\!-\!\int\!\d^3x\Big[\tr \partial_{a}\Phi\,\partial^{a}\Phi^{\dag} + \mu\,\tr\Phi\Phi^{\dag} \\
L000577	    +\, \lambda_1\!\left(\tr\Phi\Phi^{\dag}\right)^{\!2} \!+\lambda_2\tr\!\left(\Phi\Phi^{\dag}\right)^{\!2} \!- \!\lambda_3\left(\det\!\Phi + \text{c.c.}\right)\!\Big]\!\Big\},
L000578	\end{gathered}
L000579	\end{align}
L000580	where $\Phi(x)$ is an $N_f$-by-$N_f$ complex matrix field, with the symmetry~\eqref{eq:3D_IR_symmetry} acting on $\Phi(x)$ in the same way as $\phi(x)$.
L000581	The question is whether this model hits a natural critical point as $\mu$ varies.
L000582	
L000583	\footnote{One may speculate that the model~\eqref{eq:Landau-Ginzburg} flows to a fixed point with emergent $U(1)_A$ symmetry, with the $\lambda_3$ term irrelevant~\cite{Pisarski:1983ms,Cohen:1996ng,Lee:1996zy,Cohen:2002st,Fukushima:2008wg,Aoki:2012yj,Pelissetto:2013hqa,Cossu:2013uua}.
L000584	However, as discussed in the Supplemental Material~\ref{sec:U1A}, no convincing evidence supports this possibility in the model~\eqref{eq:Landau-Ginzburg}.
L000585	}
L000586	
L000587	
L000588	When $N_f=2$, the model~\eqref{eq:Landau-Ginzburg} at mean-field level undergoes an $O(4)$ second-order transition
L000589	at $\mu=|\lambda_3|$.
L000590	This critical point survive fluctuations and flows to the $O(4)$ Wilson-Fisher universality class~\cite{Pisarski:1983ms,Butti:2003nu}, as expected since the symmetry~\eqref{eq:3D_IR_symmetry} is $O(4)\rtimes(\Z_2)_{\calR}$ at $\theta_B=0,\pi$, and $SO(4)\rtimes(\Z_2)_{\calR}$ for $\theta_B\neq0,\pi$.
L000591	Hence the Landau and Landau-DQCP scenarios are both plausible for $N_f=2$.
L000592	
L000593	At mean-field level, the $\lambda_3$ term drives the transition first-order for $N_f=3$, but allows an $O(2N_f^2)$ second-order transition at $\mu=0$ for $N_f\ge4$.
L000594	Unfortunately, beyond mean-field level, extensive searches have found no stable fixed points in the model~\eqref{eq:Landau-Ginzburg} for all $N_f\ge 3$~\cite{Pisarski:1983ms,Butti:2003nu,Calabrese:2004uk,Adzhemyan:2021sug,Fejos:2014qga,Resch:2017vjs,Fejos:2022mso,Fejos:2024bgl}.
L000595	We thus consider the Landau and Landau-DQCP scenarios to be disfavored for $N_f\geq 3$.
L000596	
L000597	
L000598	
L000599	
L000600	{\bf Conformal manifold.}
L000601	
L000602	
L000603	The only option not yet disfavored for $N_f\ge3$ is the
L000604	conformal-manifold scenario, which might work for $N_f=2$ as well. We
L000605	denote the CFT at $\theta_B$ on the critical line by
L000606	$SU(N_f)_{\theta_B}$. We obtain a non-conformal field theory by adding
L000607	the deformation
L000608	\begin{equation}
L000609	   \mu \int\d^3x\ \calO_E\,,
L000610	\end{equation}
L000611	to the $SU(N_f)_{\theta_{B}}$ CFT. This $SU(N_f)_{\theta_B}$ field theory must
L000612	describe the 3D IR physics of finite-$T$ massless QCD for all $T$. It should
L000613	flow to a gapped phase for $\mu>0$ and a Nambu-Goldstone
L000614	phase for $\mu<0$.
L000615	
L000616	Whether the $SU(N_f)_{\theta_B}$ CFT exists is a nontrivial question, since
L000617	non-supersymmetric conformal manifolds are rare in
L000618	3D~\cite{Gaberdiel:2008fn,Bashmakov:2017rko,Behan:2017mwi,Hollands:2017chb,Sen:2017gfr,Giambrone:2021wsm}.
L000619	We now argue for the existence of the $SU(N_f)_0$ CFT that generates the
L000620	conformal manifold via $\calO_B$ deformations.
L000621	
L000622	If we can arbitrarily ``contract'' the $\mu<0$ RG flow into its IR end by smoothly
L000623	deforming the $SU(N_f)_0$ field theory, we can study a deformed version of the $SU(N_f)_0$ CFT via
L000624	loop expansions around the Gaussian IR fixed point. The IR tail of
L000625	the $\mu<0$ RG flow is described by the 3D $SU(N_f)$ principal chiral model
L000626	(PCM) of Eq.~\eqref{eq:lowT_EFT}.
L000627	Conveniently, loop expansions in the $(2+\epsilon)$D $SU(N_f)$ PCM with
L000628	arbitrarily small $\epsilon>0$ are known to exhibit an arbitrarily short
L000629	RG flow from an interacting ultraviolet (UV) fixed point to the Gaussian
L000630	IR fixed point~\cite{ZinnJustin_book}. It is tempting to view this UV
L000631	fixed point as a weakly-coupled avatar of the $SU(N_f)_0$ CFT.
L000632	
L000633	The $(2+\epsilon)$D UV fixed point should contain the avatar of the
L000634	exactly marginal operator $\calO_B$.
L000635	The key observation~\cite{Nahum:2015jya,Jones:2024ept,DeCesare:2025ukl,DeCesare:2026dwm}
L000636	is that a $d$-dimensional QFT with $d\in\R_+$ is like an
L000637	infinite-dimensional QFT, since it is formally an analytic continuation
L000638	of a family of $n$-dimensional QFTs for all $n\in\Z_+$. The
L000639	$(2+\epsilon)$D $SU(N_f)$ PCM thus sees the topology of the $SU(N_f)$
L000640	target space in all finite dimensions, including $\pi_3(SU(N_f))=\Z$.
L000641	As a result, at the $(2+\epsilon)$D UV fixed point, the closed Skyrme
L000642	3-form has protected scaling dimension exactly $3$ and generates a short
L000643	conformal multiplet~\cite{DeCesare:2025ukl}. The $(2+\epsilon)$D Skyrme 3-form is thus a
L000644	promising avatar for $\calO_B$.
L000645	
L000646	A large-$N$ analysis~\cite{DeCesare:2025ukl} indicates that if the 3D
L000647	$SU(2)_0$ CFT exists, it might be close to the 3D $O(4)$ Wilson-Fisher
L000648	CFT, so existing lattice measurements~\cite{Philipsen:2021qji} might not
L000649	distinguish them. The idea that fixed points in $2+\epsilon$ and $4-\epsilon$
L000650	dimensions could have different extrapolations to 3D has recently been
L000651	highlighted by De Cesare and Rychkov~\cite{DeCesare:2025ukl,DeCesare:2026dwm}.
L000652	
L000653	To see whether the $(2+\epsilon)$D UV fixed point extrapolates to
L000654	$\epsilon=1$, we study the scaling dimensions of $\calO_E$ and the lowest-dimension
L000655	primary scalar $\calO_{\phi}$ in the
L000656	$\boldsymbol{N_f}\otimes\overline{\boldsymbol{N_f}}$ representation using
L000657	the three-loop results of
L000658	Refs.~\cite{McKane:1979cm,Hikami:1980hi,Hikami:1982ak} and the four-loop results of Ref.~\cite{Wegner:1989ss}:
L000659	\begin{subequations}
L000660	\begin{align}
L000661	    \Delta_E &= 2 - \frac{1}{2}\epsilon^2 - \frac{1}{4}\epsilon^3
L000662	    - \left( \frac{5}{16} + \frac{9\zeta_3}{4N_f^2} \right)\epsilon^4
L000663	    + \calO(\epsilon^5),\\
L000664	    \Delta_{\phi} &= \left(1-\frac{1}{N_f^2}\right)\left[\epsilon
L000665	    - \frac{1}{2}  \epsilon^2 +  \frac{1}{2} \epsilon^3\right]
L000666	    +\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon ^4\right).
L000667	\end{align}
L000668	\end{subequations}
L000669	We estimate $\Delta_E$ and $\Delta_{\phi}$ in the 3D $SU(N_f)_0$ CFT using
L000670	Pad\'e-Borel resummation; see the Supplemental Material~\ref{sec:epsilon_extrapolation}.
L000671	We summarize our
L000672	3D estimates with small $N_f$ in Table~\ref{tab:Delta_phi_estimates}, which are consistent with unitarity $\Delta_E,\Delta_{\phi} \ge \frac{1}{2}$ and relevance $\Delta_E,\Delta_{\phi} < 3$.
L000673	More terms in the $(2+\epsilon)$D expansion are needed to reduce the extrapolation errors, which are significant for $\Delta_{E}$.
