L000794	\section[Effective theory in the high-T gapped phase]{Effective theory in the high-$T$ gapped phase}
L000795	\label{sec:high_T_EFT}
L000796	
L000797	
L000798	
L000799	
L000800	According to perturbative calculations~\cite{KapustaGale201102, Roberge:1986mm}, the high-$T$ (i.e.~small-$\beta$) phase of massless QCD is gapped for all $\theta_B$.
L000801	The free energy density $f_{\beta}(\theta_B)$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:free_energy} at small $\beta$ is an even $2\pi$-periodic analytic function for every $\theta_B$ except at $\theta_B=\pi$ (mod $2\pi$), where it has a cusp-like maximum.
L000802	All the propagating degrees of freedom are gapped out at distance scales $\ell \gg \beta$, so a 3D effective field theory at $\ell \gg \beta$ describes the colorless vacua only.
L000803	Such a 3D QFT is not completely trivial because the theory is not uniformly trivially gapped for all $\theta_B$.
L000804	It has a path-integral description in terms of two colorless fields, a real scalar field $\varphi$ and a 2-form $U(1)$ gauge field $b$ \cite{Kallosh:1995hi,Gabadadze:1999na,Gabadadze:2000vw,Gabadadze:2002ff,Dvali:2005an,Burgess:2023ifd}:
L000805	\begin{equation}\label{eq:high_T_EFT}
L000806	    \int\calD b\calD\varphi \exp\left\{ -\int\d^3x\,
L000807	    \bar{f}_{\beta}(\varphi) + \frac{i}{2\pi} \int\varphi\d b\right\}\,.
L000808	\end{equation}
L000809	The above $\bar{f}_{\beta}(\varphi)$ is an even analytic function of $\varphi$ such that
L000810	\begin{equation}
L000811	    \bar{f}_{\beta}(\varphi)
L000812	    \begin{cases}
L000813	        = f_{\beta}(\varphi), & -\pi\le\varphi\le\pi \\
L000814	        > f_{\beta}(\varphi), & \text{for other }\varphi
L000815	    \end{cases}.
L000816	\end{equation}
L000817	This 3D model has a $2$-form $U(1)$ symmetry on $b$'s electric side and a $(-1)$-form $U(1)$ symmetry on $b$'s magnetic side.
L000818	With a background $3$-form $U(1)$ gauge field $C$ and a background $0$-form $U(1)$ gauge field (i.e.~a background $2\pi$-periodic scalar) $\theta_B$, the path integral reads
L000819	\begin{equation}\label{eq:high_T_EFT_background}
L000820	    \int\calD b\calD\varphi \exp\left\{ -\int\d^3x\,
L000821	    \bar{f}_{\beta}(\varphi) + \frac{i}{2\pi} \int (\varphi - \theta_B)(\d b-C)\right\}\,.
L000822	\end{equation}
L000823	This 3D model has no propagating degrees of freedom and describes infinitely many vacua (sometimes called ``universes''~\cite{Hellerman:2006zs,Sharpe:2014tca,Tanizaki:2019rbk,Komargodski:2020mxz,Cherman:2020cvw,Lin:2025oml,Sharpe:2022ene}) labeled by the $2$-form $U(1)$ symmetry charge $n\in\Z$.
L000824	For constant $\theta_B$, each vacuum has the energy density given by $\bar{f}_{\beta}(2\pi n + \theta_B)$, and two vacua cross at $\theta_B=\pi$.
L000825	The $2$-form $U(1)$ symmetry has to be emergent and only the lowest vacua are relevant for high-$T$ massless QCD.
L000826	
L000827	To match the 3D 't Hooft anomaly~\eqref{eq:SPT_3D}, the high-$T$ effective theory~\eqref{eq:high_T_EFT} is supposed to see the full 3D symmetry~\eqref{eq:3D_IR_symmetry}.
L000828	The actions of discrete symmetries on the high-$T$ effective theory~\eqref{eq:high_T_EFT} are evident:
L000829	\begin{equation}
L000830	    \varphi(x)\xrightarrow{\calC}-\varphi(x)\,,\
L000831	    b(x)\xrightarrow{\calC}-b(x)\,,\qquad
L000832	    \varphi(x)\xrightarrow{\calS}-\varphi(x)\,,\
L000833	    b(x)\xrightarrow{\calS}-b(x)\,,\qquad
L000834	    \varphi(x)\xrightarrow{\calR}\varphi(\overline{x})\,,\
L000835	    b(x)\xrightarrow{\calR}-\overline{b}(\overline{x})\,.
L000836	\end{equation}
L000837	At first glance, it seems that there is no room for the $SU(N_f)_{L,R}$ symmetry to act, since Eq.~\eqref{eq:high_T_EFT_background} has no continuous 0-form symmetry of any sort.
L000838	Nevertheless, the emergent $2$-form $U(1)$ symmetry can be a homomorphism target of the $0$-form $SU(N_f)_{L,R}$ symmetry.
L000839	Namely, the $A_{L,R}$ gauge fields can be coupled via the substitution
L000840	\begin{equation}\label{eq:C=A^2}
L000841	    C = \frac{1}{4\pi}\tr\!\left( A_L \d A_L - A_R \d A_R
L000842	    - \i\frac{2}{3} A_L^3 + \i\frac{2}{3} A_R^3 \right).
L000843	\end{equation}
L000844	The emergent $2$-form $U(1)$ symmetry has a mixed 't Hooft anomaly with the $(-1)$-form $U(1)$ symmetry, described by the 4D invertible theory
L000845	\begin{equation}\label{eq:high_T_anomaly}
L000846	    \exp\left\{\frac{i}{2\pi}\int\theta_B\d C\right\}.
L000847	\end{equation}
L000848	This emergent anomaly precisely reproduces the 3D anomaly~\eqref{eq:SPT_3D} via the substitution~\eqref{eq:C=A^2}.
L000849	Mathematically, such a homomorphism from $0$-form $SU(N_f)_{L,R}$ to $2$-form $U(1)$ is encoded in the classifying-space fibration $BSU(N_f)\to B^3U(1)$ that induces an isomorphism from $\pi_4(BSU(N_f))=\Z$ to $\pi_4(B^3U(1))=\Z$.
L000850	
L000851	The above way of matching anomalies via flowing to an emergent higher-form symmetry was recently packaged into the notion of symmetry transmutation~\cite{Seiberg:2025bqy}.
L000852	So we can say that the $0$-form $SU(N_f)_{L,R}$ chiral symmetry of QCD is transmuted into a $2$-form $U(1)$ symmetry in its high-$T$ effective theory.
L000853	
L000854	
L000855	
L000856	
L000857	
L000858	\section[Second-order phase transition at theta=pi in the high-T phase]{Second-order phase transition at $\theta_B=\pi$ in the high-$T$ phase}
L000859	\label{sec:anomalons}
L000860	
L000861	
L000862	
L000863	
L000864	In the high-$T$ phase, a window of second-order phase transitions at $\theta_B=\pi$ would have a chance to be realized at relatively lower $T$, if a 3D CFT consistent with symmetry and anomaly existed.
L000865	The upper edge of this gapless window would be a tricritical point, while the lower edge could be connected to the DQCP or the $SU(N_f)_{\pi}$ CFT on the critical line of chiral transitions.
L000866	
L000867	One may speculate that such an appropriate 3D CFT can simply be provided by colorless free fermions, because the 3D anomaly~\eqref{eq:SPT_3D} can be matched by an $N_f$-flavor free massless 4D Dirac fermion at finite temperatures.
L000868	At $\theta_B=\pi$, one Matsubara mode becomes massless, leading to the 3D effective theory at $\ell\gg\beta$:
L000869	\begin{align}\label{eq:anomalon}
L000870	    \int\calD\psi\calD\bar{\psi}\calD\chi\calD\bar{\chi}
L000871	    \ \exp\left\{-\int d^{3}x\, \Big(\bar{\psi}\slashed{\partial}\psi +\bar{\chi}\slashed{\partial}\chi\Big)\right\}\,,
L000872	    \qquad(\theta_B=\pi)
L000873	\end{align}
L000874	with two $N_f$-flavor free massless 3D Dirac fermions $\psi$ and $\chi$.
L000875	At $\theta_B\neq\pi$, all the Matsubara modes are massive and these 3D fermions are gapped out at $\ell\gg\beta$.
L000876	This speculation has an interplay with the Landau-DQCP scenario.
L000877	Around the chiral transition, these 3D fermions have to talk to the chiral condensate $\Phi$ through Yukawa couplings,
L000878	\begin{equation}
L000879	    y_1\int d^3x \left(\bar{\psi} \Phi \Phi^{\dag} \psi - \bar{\chi} \Phi^{\dag} \Phi \chi\right)\,,
L000880	    \qquad
L000881	    y_2\int d^3x \left(\bar{\psi}\Phi\chi + \bar{\chi}\Phi^{\dag}\psi \right)\,,
L000882	    \qquad\text{etc.}
L000883	\end{equation}
L000884	Therefore, the DQCP at $\theta_B=\pi$ might be described by a certain 3D Gross-Neveu-Yukawa universality class, as long as the gapless window exists and has a free-fermion description.
L000885	
L000886	Nevertheless, as already pointed out by Ref.~\cite{Kobayashi:2023ajk}, although it matches the anomaly~\eqref{eq:SPT_3D} of QCD, the free-fermion CFT~\eqref{eq:anomalon} does not match the symmetry~\eqref{eq:QCD_symmetry} of QCD.
L000887	A colorless fermionic object in the fundamental representation of $SU(N_f)_L$ or in the anti-fundamental representation of $SU(N_f)_R$ can exist in QCD only when
L000888	\begin{equation}
L000889	    N_c = 1 \mod N_f
L000890	    \qquad\text{and}\qquad
L000891	    N_c = 1 \mod 2
L000892	\end{equation}
L000893	When this happens, this colorless fermionic object necessarily carries a baryon number $q_B$ satisfying $q_B = 1 \mod N_f$.
L000894	For example, with physical $N_c=3$, the above conditions are satisfied by $N_f=2$, but are violated by $N_f=3$.
L000895	Therefore, a gapless window at $\theta_B=\pi$ described by the free-fermion CFT~\eqref{eq:anomalon} is at least conceivable for $N_f=2$, but is immediately excluded for $N_f=3$.
L000896	Of course, a more complicated free-fermion CFT might simultaneously match the symmetry and the anomaly, and we leave a detailed exploration of such possibilities to the future.
L000897	
L000898	
L000899	
L000900	
L000901	\section[The fate of emergent U(1)A symmetry at the chiral transition]{The fate of emergent $U(1)_A$ symmetry at the chiral transition}
L000902	\label{sec:U1A}
L000903	
L000904	
L000905	
L000906	
L000907	It is conceivable that a $U(1)_A$ symmetry emerges on the critical line in Landau scenarios~\cite{Cohen:1996ng,Lee:1996zy,Cohen:2002st,Fukushima:2008wg,Aoki:2012yj,Aoki:2012yj,Pelissetto:2013hqa,Cossu:2013uua,Pisarski:2024esv}.
L000908	For this to happen, two requirements are needed: (I) the Ginzburg-Landau model~\eqref{eq:Landau-Ginzburg} with $\lambda_3=0$ must have an IR fixed point and (II) $\det\Phi$ must be an irrelevant operator at that fixed point.
L000909	Establishing (I) is already highly nontrivial since the $\lambda_2$ fluctuations destabilize the mean-field $O(2N_f^2)$ critical point.
L000910	
L000911	When $N_f=2$, the model~\eqref{eq:Landau-Ginzburg} with $\lambda_3=0$ may flow to some Wilson-Fisher-like $O(4)\times O(2)$ fixed point~\cite{Pelissetto:2013hqa,Nakayama:2014sba,Henriksson:2020fqi} (see also~Refs.~\cite{Yabunaka:2017uox,Yabunaka:2018mju,Sorokin:2021jwf,Sorokin:2022zwh,Sanchez-Villalobos:2024vmd}).
L000912	But $\det\Phi\sim\Phi^2$ is relevant at that fixed point, since it
L000913	is literally a mass term.
L000914	When $N_f\ge3$, there is no convincing evidence for the existence of fixed points in the model~\eqref{eq:Landau-Ginzburg} with $\lambda_3=0$~\cite{Pisarski:1983ms,Butti:2003nu,Calabrese:2004uk,Adzhemyan:2021sug,Fejos:2014qga,Resch:2017vjs,Fejos:2022mso,Fejos:2024bgl}.
L000915	We thus regard a second-order chiral transition with emergent $U(1)_A$ as disfavored in massless QCD for all $N_f\ge 2$ given current evidence.
L000916	
L000917	Further searches for fixed points in the model~\eqref{eq:Landau-Ginzburg} with $\lambda_3=0$ using lattice Monte Carlo and other methods would still be valuable.
L000918	This is because if such a fixed point existed, $\det\Phi\sim\Phi^{N_f}$ should be irrelevant for sufficiently large $N_f$, and thus $U(1)_A$ would have a chance to emerge at QCD chiral transition with sufficiently large $N_f$.
L000919	Nevertheless, it is hard to imagine that anything like this can happen for $N_f=3$, since it seems very difficult to get $\det\Phi\sim\Phi^3$ to be irrelevant.
L000920	
L000921	
L000922	
L000923	
L000924	\section[Extrapolating from (2+epsilon)D to 3D]{Extrapolating from $(2+\epsilon)$D to $3$D}
L000925	\label{sec:epsilon_extrapolation}
L000926	
L000927	
L000928	
L000929	The beta function of the $(2+\epsilon)$D $SU(N_f)$ PCM is known up to the four-loop order~\cite{Hikami:1980hi,Hikami:1982ak,Wegner:1989ss} (their coupling $t$ in the Lagrangian is related to our $g^2$ through $g^2=4t$):
L000930	\begin{align}
L000931	\begin{split}
L000932	    \beta(g^2) \ =&\: \ \epsilon g^2 -\frac{1}{4} N_f g^4
L000933	    -\frac{1}{32} N_f^2 g^6-\frac{3}{512} N_f^3 g^8 - \frac{1}{1024}\left[\frac{19}{12}N_f^4 + 3\zeta_3N_f^2 \right]g^{10}
L000934	    + \calO\left(g^{12}\right)\,,
L000935	\end{split}
L000936	\end{align}
L000937	where $\zeta_3\approx 1.202$ is the Riemann zeta function evaluated at $3$.
L000938	The UV fixed point is thus located at
L000939	\begin{equation}
L000940	    g_*^2\ =\ \frac{4}{N_f}\epsilon - \frac{2}{N_f}\epsilon^2 + \frac{1}{2N_f}\epsilon^3
L000941	    - \left(\frac{1}{3N_f}
L000942	    +\frac{3\zeta_3}{N_f^3}\right)\epsilon^4
L000943	    + \calO(\epsilon^5)
L000944	\end{equation}
L000945	The scaling dimension of the energy operator $\calO_E$ is related to the critical exponent $\nu$ that governs the divergence of the correlation length as the UV fixed point:
L000946	\begin{equation}\label{eq:Delta_energy}
L000947	\begin{split}
L000948	    \Delta_E(\epsilon)
L000949	    \ =\
L000950	    2+\epsilon - \frac{1}{\nu}
L000951	    \ =\
L000952	    2 + \epsilon +\beta'(g^2_{*}) \ =\
L000953	    2 - \frac{1}{2}\epsilon^2 - \frac{1}{4}\epsilon^3
L000954	    - \left( \frac{5}{16} + \frac{9\zeta_3}{4N_f^2} \right)\epsilon^4
L000955	    + \calO(\epsilon^5)
L000956	    \,.
L000957	\end{split}
L000958	\end{equation}
L000959	This series appears to be non-alternating. Curiously, $N_f$-dependence only
L000960	shows up from the fourth order.
L000961	
L000962	The anomalous dimension of the vertex operator $\calO_{\phi}$ in the $\boldsymbol{N_f}\otimes\overline{\boldsymbol{N_f}}$ representation of $SU(N_f)_L\times SU(N_f)_R$ is known up to the three-loop order~\cite{McKane:1979cm} (their coupling $g^2$ in the Lagrangian is the same as ours):
L000963	\begin{align}
L000964	    \gamma_{\phi}(g^2) \  = \ \frac{\left(N_f^2-1\right)}{4 N_f} \left(g^2
L000965	    + \frac{3 N_f^2}{128}  g^6\right) +\calO\left(g^8\right)\,.
L000966	\end{align}
L000967	This $\gamma_{\phi}$ is the anomalous dimension with respect to the engineering dimension $0$.
L000968	Therefore, this $\gamma_{\phi}$ is equivalent to $\frac{1}{2}\zeta$ used in Ref.~\cite[Section~4.1.3]{Henriksson:2022rnm} (see especially their Footnote~35) for the $O(N)$ nonlinear sigma model.
L000969	Hence the scaling dimension of $\phi$ at the UV fixed point is given by
L000970	\begin{align}
L000971	    \label{eq:Delta_phi}
L000972	    \Delta_{\phi}(\epsilon)
L000973	    \ =\ \gamma_{\phi}(g_*^2)
L000974	    \ = \ \left(1-\frac{1}{N_f^2}\right)\left[\epsilon
L000975	    - \frac{1}{2}  \epsilon^2 +  \frac{1}{2} \epsilon^3\right]
L000976	    +\calO\left(\epsilon ^4\right)  \,.
L000977	\end{align}
L000978	This series appears to be alternating.
L000979	The factorization of the $N_f$-dependence is unlikely to hold from the fourth order.
L000980	
L000981	To extrapolate $\Delta_E(\epsilon)$ and $\Delta_{\phi}(\epsilon)$ to $\epsilon=1$, we need to deal with a standard challenge.
L000982	Since there are no non-trivial fixed points for $\epsilon<0$, Dyson's
L000983	argument~\cite{Dyson:1952tj} suggests that the perturbative expansion in $\epsilon$
L000984	should be asymptotic.  Simply plugging $\epsilon = 1$ into a truncated series representation of $\Delta_E(\epsilon)$ and $\Delta_{\phi}(\epsilon)$ is thus likely to be a very poor approximation.  However, it is natural to expect observables like
L000985	$\Delta_{E}(\epsilon), \Delta_{\phi}(\epsilon)$ to have resurgent transseries
L000986	representations around $\epsilon = 0$, see e.g.~Ref.~\cite{Aniceto:2018bis} for
L000987	a review.  The standard way to extrapolate asymptotic series is via Borel
L000988	summation of an analytic continuation of the Borel transform of the series. Here
L000989	we will adopt the
L000990	simplest and most widely used analytic continuation, which is via Pad\'e
L000991	approximants, which are rational functions that match the series expansion to a
L000992	given order.  We will use this Pad\'e-Borel (PB) resummation approach to estimate the values of $\Delta_E(\epsilon)$ and $\Delta_{\phi}(\epsilon)$ at $\epsilon=1$.  To estimate the errors on our estimates, we will use combinations of the variation of our results as we vary the choice of Pad\'e approximant together  with the non-perturbative ambiguities in the resummations (when such ambiguities appear).
L000993	
L000994	
L000995	
L000996	
L000997	
L000998	\subsection[Extrapolation of Delta-energy]{Extrapolation of $\Delta_E$}
L000999	\label{sec:delta_energy}
L001000	
L001001	
L001002	
L001003	Replacing $\epsilon^n$ with $\delta^n/n!$, we obtain the Borel transform of the truncated $\Delta_E(\epsilon)$ series~\eqref{eq:Delta_energy}:
L001004	\begin{align}\label{eq:Borel_energy}
L001005	    \calB[\Delta_E](\delta)\ = 2-\frac{\delta^2}{4}
L001006	    - \frac{\delta^3}{24}
L001007	    - \left( \frac{5}{16} + \frac{9\zeta_3}{4N_f^2} \right)\frac{\delta^4}{24}
L001008	    +\calO\left(\delta^5\right)\,.
L001009	\end{align}
L001010	If $\Delta_E(\epsilon)$ is indeed non-alternating, we would expect $\calB[\Delta_E](\delta)$ to have a leading singularity on the positive real axis.
L001011	Such a singularity leads to imaginary ambiguities in the Borel integral that must be canceled by appropriate nonperturbative contributions in a resurgent transseries representation of $\Delta_E(\epsilon)$.
L001012	
L001013	An $[m/n]$ Pad\'e approximant to $\calB[\Delta_E](\delta)$ is a degree-$(m,n)$ rational function that matches the series expansion of $\calB[\Delta_E](\delta)$ to order $\delta^{m+n}$.
L001014	The near-diagonal Pad\'e approximants at order $\delta^4$ are
L001015	\begin{subequations}
L001016	\begin{align}
L001017	    \calB[\Delta_E](\delta)\big|_{[3/1]} &=
L001018	    \frac{2 - \Bigl(\frac{5}{8}\!+\!\frac{9\zeta_3}{2N_f^2}\Bigr)\delta - \frac{1}{4}\delta^2 + \Bigl(\frac{7}{192}\!+\!\frac{9\zeta_3}{16N_f^2}\Bigr)\delta^3 }
L001019	    {1 - \Bigl(\frac{5}{16}\!+\!\frac{9\zeta_3}{4N_f^2}\Bigr)\delta}\,,\\
L001020	    \calB[\Delta_E](\delta)\big|_{[2/2]} &=
L001021	    \frac{2 - \frac{1}{3}\delta - \Bigl(\frac{43}{144}\!+\!\frac{3\zeta_3}{4N_f^2}\Bigr)\delta^2}
L001022	    {1 - \frac{1}{6}\delta - \Bigl(\frac{7}{288}\!+\!\frac{3\zeta_3}{8N_f^2}\Bigr)\delta^2}\,,\\
L001023	    \calB[\Delta_E](\delta)\big|_{[1/3]} &=
L001024	    \frac{2 - \Bigl(\frac{17}{8}\!+\!\frac{9\zeta_3}{2N_f^2}\Bigr)\delta }
L001025	    {1 - \Bigl(\frac{17}{16}\!+\!\frac{9\zeta_3}{4N_f^2}\Bigr)\delta + \frac{1}{8}\delta^2 - \Bigl(\frac{43}{384}\!+\!\frac{9\zeta_3}{32N_f^2}\Bigr)\delta^3}\,.
L001026	\end{align}
L001027	\end{subequations}
L001028	All of them have poles on the positive real axis.
L001029	We then obtain the $[m/n]$ PB-resummed function $\Delta_E(\epsilon)$ by performing the Borel integral,
L001030	\begin{align}
L001031	    \Delta_E(\epsilon)\big|_{[m/n]} =\  \lim_{\theta\to0}\  \frac{1}{\epsilon}\int_0^{\infty} d \delta\, \exp\left(-\delta e^{i \theta}/\epsilon \right)\, \calB[\Delta_E]( \delta e^{i \theta} )\big|_{[m/n]}\,.
L001032	\end{align}
L001033	In a resurgent transseries representation of $\Delta_E(\epsilon)$, $\im \Delta_E(\epsilon)\big|_{[m/n]}$ would be canceled by a corresponding imaginary part of a non-perturbative term in the transseries, which would also give a real contribution of approximately the same magnitude.  We thus use the magnitude of the imaginary ambiguities in the Pad\'e-Borel resummations as an estimate of the error on our extrapolations.
L001034	
L001035	
L001036	We show the functions $\Delta_E(\epsilon)|_{[m/n]}$ with $N_f=2\sim7$ in Fig.~\ref{fig:Pade_Borel_E}.
L001037	We summarize our $\epsilon=1$ results as a function of $[m/n]$ and $N_f$ in Table~\ref{tab:Pade_sums_E}.
L001038	We use the union of the envelopes of $\Delta_E(1)|_{[3/1]}$, $\Delta_E(1)|_{[2/2]}$, and $\Delta_E(1)|_{[1/3]}$ to determine our estimation of 3D $\Delta_E$ and the extrapolation error, as we summarize in Table~\ref{tab:Pade_sums_E'}.
L001039	The extrapolation errors are large with the currently available four-loop data, and thus higher-loop data would be very valuable.
